<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avalokiteśvara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Avalokiteshvara, Lokeśvara, Chenrezig, Guanyin, Kannon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avalokiteśvara, the Lord who looks down, is a bodhisattva of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> associated with great compassion, known in Tibetan as Chenrezig and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> as Guanyin, Kannon or Gwaneum. Chapter 25 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> describes him as a bodhisattva who hears the cries of beings and helps all who call on his name, in 33 manifestations, some of them female. He is depicted in a vast number of forms across Buddhist cultures, and his mantra, Oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ, is the most popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. He is the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> figure who entered the worship of ordinary Buddhists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> countries, and many Tibetan Buddhists hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> to be his manifestations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara.jpg" alt="Avalokiteśvara" width="330" height="524" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyougushi · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khasarpana_Lokesvara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
